    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking into a zoning issue in Austin for a client. We have a parcel currently zoned as R-1 (Single-Family Residential), but the client wants to reclassify it to a mixed-use designation to allow for a small retail component. I'm tripping over some conflicting language in the municipal code regarding \"encroachments\" from adjacent lots and how they affect the setback requirements for new construction. Can you help me outline a strategy for navigating these conflicting clauses during the variance application process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To navigate these conflicting clauses, focus on the specific geographic definition of \"encroachment\" in the Texas Zoning Code versus Austin's local ordinances, as definitions vary significantly between the two. You should prepare a detailed variance request that demonstrates how the proposed setback reduction or exemption directly benefits the new mixed-use purpose rather than appearing as an arbitrary deviation. Before filing, carefully review the legislative history of the relevant sections to craft an argument that either clarifies the intended scope or highlights inappropriateness in applying those specific rules to current zoning issues.",
